WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.961
Every electric car battery needs lithium.

2
00:00:03.311 --> 00:00:06.893
And California believes it's sitting on a huge supply, under a

3
00:00:06.893 --> 00:00:08.858
desert lake called the Salton Sea.

4
00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:12.389
The question is whether we can get it out without making the air

5
00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.179
worse for the people who live there.

6
00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:18.918
The Salton Sea is California's largest lake, in the southern desert.

7
00:00:19.268 --> 00:00:21.647
The lithium here isn't dug out of rock.

8
00:00:21.647 --> 00:00:24.879
It's dissolved in hot, salty brine, deep underground.

9
00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:28.993
Geothermal plants already pump that brine up to make electricity.

10
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:32.640
A new step would pull the lithium out, then send the brine back

11
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:32.930
down.

12
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.738
But there's a catch.

13
00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:36.219
The Salton Sea is shrinking.

14
00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.990
As the water pulls back, it leaves dry lakebed behind.

15
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.905
And on windy days, that lakebed turns to dust.

16
00:00:43.255 --> 00:00:45.289
Lithium plants need water too.

17
00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:49.697
In August 2026, a court ruled the biggest project's review hadn't

18
00:00:49.697 --> 00:00:52.748
shown how its water use could affect the air.

19
00:00:53.498 --> 00:00:54.943
So who breathes that air?

20
00:00:54.943 --> 00:00:55.695
Let's map it.

21
00:00:56.045 --> 00:00:59.762
California scores every census tract for health and pollution

22
00:00:59.762 --> 00:01:00.188
burden.

23
00:01:00.188 --> 00:01:02.808
Around the sea, that's seventy-nine tracts.

24
00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:07.252
Twenty of them rank in the state's worst fifteen percent for asthma

25
00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:08.291
emergency visits.

26
00:01:08.291 --> 00:01:10.369
All twenty are in Imperial County.

27
00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:12.640
Next, the dust itself.

28
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.859
Eight EPA monitors around the sea measure coarse dust, called

29
00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:17.412
P M ten.

30
00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:22.516
From 2023 through 2025, at least one of them went over the federal

31
00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:25.613
health limit on seventy-one different days.

32
00:01:25.963 --> 00:01:28.400
Bad days peak in spring and early fall.

33
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.336
But a monitor can't tell if the dust came from the lakebed, the

34
00:01:32.336 --> 00:01:33.835
desert, or a farm field.

35
00:01:34.585 --> 00:01:36.499
This is where GeoAI comes in:

36
00:01:36.499 --> 00:01:40.260
artificial intelligence that learns from maps and images.

37
00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:44.565
Drones can't extract lithium, but they can watch the ground, flying

38
00:01:44.565 --> 00:01:47.990
low to label which lakebed is crusted, and which is loose.

39
00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:52.020
A model learns from those labels, then applies them to satellite

40
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.802
images of the entire shoreline.

41
00:01:54.152 --> 00:01:54.690
The goal:

42
00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.632
add wind data, and forecast bad dust days one to three days ahead,

43
00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:00.723
so schools and clinics can prepare.

44
00:02:01.473 --> 00:02:05.206
But a model is only useful if it works in places it hasn't seen.

45
00:02:05.556 --> 00:02:09.493
So instead of testing on random points, we hold out whole stretches

46
00:02:09.493 --> 00:02:10.257
of shoreline.

47
00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:12.255
If it fails there, it isn't ready.

48
00:02:13.005 --> 00:02:15.693
Then we compare solutions, downsides included.

49
00:02:16.043 --> 00:02:19.752
At Owens Lake, flooding the dry lakebed cut dust by more than

50
00:02:19.752 --> 00:02:20.968
ninety-nine percent.

51
00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:24.859
But it cost over two and a half billion dollars, and about sixty

52
00:02:24.859 --> 00:02:26.987
thousand acre-feet of water a year.

53
00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:30.130
Gravel needs no water, but tons of rock.

54
00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:34.530
Habitat ponds help birds, but the state has finished only about

55
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.276
a sixth of its 2028 goal.

56
00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:37.775
No single fix wins.

57
00:02:37.775 --> 00:02:41.468
The models help decide which patch of lakebed gets which fix.

58
00:02:42.218 --> 00:02:44.362
Finally, the models run scenarios:

59
00:02:44.362 --> 00:02:48.651
no new lithium, one big project, or full build-out, with and without

60
00:02:48.651 --> 00:02:49.471
dust control.

61
00:02:49.821 --> 00:02:52.957
That way, we can compare futures before anything is built.

62
00:02:53.707 --> 00:02:56.560
Lithium and clean air don't have to be a trade-off.

63
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.846
But only if we measure.

64
00:02:58.196 --> 00:03:01.904
The full plan, the interactive maps, and all the code are free

65
00:03:01.904 --> 00:03:02.562
to explore.

66
00:03:02.562 --> 00:03:04.297
Links are in the description.
